<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.linux.plist

  One-shot boot job that mounts the Linux ABI filesystems (nextbsd-userland#190).

  On stock FreeBSD this is rc.d/linux's job (linux_enable=YES). NextBSD has no
  rc.d and ships no /etc/fstab, so it had no owner. /usr/libexec/nextbsd-linux
  pre-creates $emul/{proc,sys,dev} under compat.linux.emul_path (default
  /compat/linux) and always mounts linprocfs, linsysfs, devfs, fdescfs
  (linrdlnk) and a sized tmpfs on dev/shm there, whether or not a Linux
  userland is installed, exactly as rc.d/linux does. The native set (/proc,
  /sys, /dev/fd, /dev/shm on the real root) is added only if native_mounts=YES
  in /etc/nextbsd-linux.conf.

  WHY org.nextbsd.* — nothing Apple-derived looks this label up and Apple has
  no such daemon; see org.nextbsd.wland.plist for the naming rule.

  RunAtLoad, KeepAlive false: it runs once per boot, after launchctl bootstrap
  has applied /etc/sysctl.conf (so a moved compat.linux.emul_path is already in
  effect) and run any admin fstab, and exits. It is idempotent, so it is safe to
  re-run by hand after creating or repopulating a Linux root:
      launchctl start org.nextbsd.linux
  (launchd-842's launchctl has no kickstart.) The debootstrap / apt wrapper
  (nextbsd-userland#241) runs the tool directly after bootstrapping, because
  debootstrap unmounts $emul/proc on its way out.

  No WatchPaths: those keys are inert on this launchd (nextbsd-userland#243),
  and none are needed, since the mounts exist before any root is populated.

  Design: https://pkgdemon.github.io/nextbsd-linux-abi-mounts-plan.html
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.linux</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/libexec/nextbsd-linux</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/nextbsd-linux.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/nextbsd-linux.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
